Inflation in Europe has leapfrogged due to food
Accelerating inflation measured in the euro area is mainly due to a constant rise in food prices.
November price growth in the 13 eurozone countries was higher above expectations, after 2.6 per cent by annual inflation of 3.1 per cent in October, according to the Luxembourg statistics office of the Union.
The official forecast assumed only 3 percent, Bloomberg reported.
Contrary to U.S., British and Canadian issuancies, the European Central Bank (ECB) did not reduce the key interest rate, with the fact that the reduction, together with rising prices of raw materials and falling unemployment, would result in an inflationary spiral.
The ECB would like to keep the inflation rate below two percent, but near them.
According to one of the Analysts, the ECB is in an absurd stance: it must “advise” inflation, so that it does not have to do business in this matter later.
In Germany, November’s 3.3 per cent inflation represents a 15-year-reaching record, rising from 3.6 to 4.1 per cent in Spain.
Inflation in the eurozone has escalated due to skyrocketing food and energetic prices.
Whethale term prices have risen by 88 per cent, while the soybean exchange rate has reached its highest level since 1973.
Likewise, it was necessary to pay significantly more for edible oil and dairy products in 2007.
Petroleum prices on world markets have risen by 52 per cent this year, and last month it has already cost a barrel of black gold almost $100.
According to the ECB, inflation in 2008 from this year's 2.1 per cent to 2.5, but will already fall to 1.9 in 2009.
According to analysts, the 12-month inflation level in the next 3-4 months will continue to be around 3 per cent, but its average annual rate will be 2.1 per cent.
At the same time, they expect the ECB to lower interest rates twice during 2008.
