Speasurrender of last year's October unstanchments, 35 per cent had been desired by the government and the prime minister, at the moment, only 20 per cent, while the relative majority (33 per cent) sees a solution only in the announcement of new elections.
For interest in a referendum, it is symptomatic that 60 per cent of respondents responded to a query about their eventual attendance, that they would definitely come to the referendum and another 20 percent said it would probably come.
By comparison, "Parliamentary elections organized this Sunday" would certainly be attended by only 55 percent, and another 15 percent consider their turnout to be probable. The success of the referendum would be undoubted on the basis of current intentions.
Tuition fees would say no 74, doctor's fees 79, and fees for hospital stay 84 per cent.
