€400 million for infrastructure
An agreement on three credit frameworks worth €400 million in support of education, transport and environmental infrastructure as well as energy was signed by Finance Minister János Veres and Marta Gajecko, vice-president of the European Investment Bank (EIB).
The European Investment Bank (EIB) in 2006 approved a €300-million-de-speaking €30 million for Hungary's budgetary financing of transport and environmental projects carried out aid from the Coal-Gay Fund, with the parties having previously concluded a loan agreement for an amount of CZK 100 million, the now-signatted contract relates to the use of the remaining €200 million.
A €150-million-million-dollar loan agreement is also used for budgetary financing of projects related to education.
The EIB has approved a total €300-million-dollar €300 million for Hungary for these purposes, using the remaining €150 million may take place at a later date with an extension of the loan agreement.
In the field of innovation, the parties have signed a €50 million contract, which will apply the budget mainly to the University of Debrecen Research-Equivation.
There was also talks at the meeting on the 4th route of the metro.
The parties have come to an agreement that, after Hungary has dealt with the details of metro financing with the EU, it will return to whether and how the original treaty needs to be modified.
According to János Veres, this could happen in the first quarter of 2008.
The EIB has financed €8.4 billion in a total of €113 projects in Hungary since 1990.
The interest rate provided by the EIB with an eight-year waiting period and without a commitment is tied to the EURIBOR rate.
Bulgaria is the real estate paradise of the EU
The development of the Bulgarian real estate market is stagnant - the attention of the whole of Europe turns to the previously somewhat neglected Balkan country.
What, where and who is still shopping at the cheapest but rapidly making the EU market more expensive?
Analysing Re/Max of the Bulgarian Division of Re/Max.
Undoubtedly, the most sought-after Eastern European destination for real estate shopping is Bulgaria with its inexpensive ski apartments and houses on the seashore.
Of the mountains there are the most in the course of Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitoša, on the seashore then Albena, the Golden Sands, the sunny coast, the ancient cities of Sozopol and Nessebar, but more and more people are also looking for real estate around Kavarna and Balčik.
According to Analysts from Re/Max Bulgaria, sooner or later the interest of both investors and the central part of the country will arouse the interest of investors - for example Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, Triavna - and more and more bidders are also shopping around the downright cheap city of Rus.
The capital of Sophia is also the promise of tempted possibilities, business and office spaces promise the highest returns.
Bulgaria is on the best way to turn into one of Europe's most appealing golf paradises, becoming, in addition to the summer and winter season a sought-after destination for the next two seasons.
Predictable investors are already buying in bulk, for example around Bansko.
According to analysts, real estate buyers in Bulgaria are also trying to change their lives and lifestyle.
Most customers come from England, but there are also buyers from France, Belgium, Spain and, more and more and more from Romania.
Maltese and Cypriot investors are also present, there are traditionally many Russian customers by the Black Sea and in ski paradises.
Foreign buyers can be divided into three groups, says Georgiy Jankov, regional director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
Established people buy either refurbished properties worth 50-100 thousand euros or dilapidated old houses for 5-15 thousand euros, then refurbished.
Requiring good accessibility and the possibilities of communication, it is important for them the proximity of a large city, shops and, as a rule, a local doctor.
The second category is buyers of chat (Bulgaria ranked among the top 10 German holiday destinations in December).
The third group consists of commercial investors, who are buying in hopes of rising value in the mid- and long term.
Real estate prices in the capital are continuous, as the demand is getting more and stronger.
In Bulgaria, while real estate prices in the European Union are the lowest prices, they have risen by 21 per cent a year over the past five years.
Increasing real estate prices far outweigh the growth in salaries.
Fodor and Hungary's six per cent
According to the Minister of the Environment and Water Management Gábor Fodor, the meetings of experts will be concluded on Tuesday, and after Wednesday's official launch, the international conference on the climate regime after 2012 will continue with a meeting of ministers.
Experts and politicians from 192 countries in the world are discussing the Roadmap from Bali, which is addressing the efforts following the emission reduction period deduced by the Kyoto Protocol in force until 2012.
At the moment, there is a heated debate between developing and developed countries.
The U.S. has also announced that it now does not intend to negotiate precise figures, although they are leaning towards an agreement in order to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 25-40 per cent by 2020 in view of 1990.
The next big question of the conference is whether developed countries, in addition to reducing emissions, will also proceed to providing both material and technological assistance to countries reliant on it.
An agreement was also born on subsidies for developing countries.
The subsidy fund laid down by the Kyoto Protocol will be led by a 16-member Governing Board, most likely under the World Environmental Protection (GEF) Programme at the United Nations - it can be read in the journal Piac és Profit.
Hungarian six per cent
The Minister of the Environment and Water Industry Gábor Fodor, on behalf of Hungary, supports the EU's efforts to maintain a leading role in efforts for global climate protection and to facilitate the creation of a new international agreement by 2009.
The tribute argued that the EU is committing to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20 per cent by 2020 compared to the 1990 level, regardless of the success of the "degree-launch agreement", and is ready to commit to up to a 30 per cent reduction if other developed regions of the world or developed countries are willing to accept responsibilities and commitments corresponding to their capabilities.
Gábor Fodor pointed out that the countries that joined the Kyoto Protocol in 1997 set a reduction of 5.2 per cent target, while the then 15 EU Member States pledged 8 per cent.
Hungary's commitment is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 6 per cent compared to 1985-87, which our homeland will meet. - it is commemorated in a press release by the Ministry of the Environment.
There is a lot going on in Bali, because within two years it is necessary to adopt an international agreement on long-term policy of climate change, otherwise its entry into force with certainty would not be postponed until 2012. In such a case, a new system of emission reduction would not be immediately linked to the commitments to the reduction provided for by the Kyoto Protocol, whose necessity in its most recent report was also pointed out by the U.N. International Climate Change Scientific Board - the IPCC, which holds the Nobel Peace Prize.
This would lead to a delay in reducing emissions and an inevitable increase in average temperature on Earth by more than 2 degrees Celsius, an extremely serious and irrevocable consequence, according to scientists.
The British army is recruiting on YouTube
British military units are releasing footage recordings from actions in Iraq and Afghanistan to popularize themselves on video-sharing.
At first, the British Air Force (RAF) liked YouTube: special forces operating in Afghanistan since March 2006 have been uploading videos from actions against the Taliban since March 2006, according to the BBC.
The recordings, taken by a miniature camera affixed to the helmet, are controlled by the Air Force Head of Combustion in the Kandahar area and then shipled further to London, where they are transmitted to the Internet.
A political message is unavoidable
In this form there have already been 139 recordings of soldiers of the British air forces on the Internet and more than 75 thousand people have already looked at their "channel".
The British Ministry of Defense registered in the spring of 2007 as "defenceheadquarters" and publishes recordings from actions in both Iraq and Afghanistan, while the British Foreign Ministry (Commonwealth Office) and international peacekeepers in Iraq (MNFI) have their own channels on YouTube.
Commitment in Afghanistan
Aerial forces have released videos according to their original intentions for the purpose of recruition, and according to the Ministry of Defense, they are also good at familiarizing the general public with the real goal of the presence of an army abroad.
Promotation, published in this form compared to traditional televised reciprocating advertisements, costing almost nothing,” defense expert Gordon Mackenzie told the BBC, “but it necessarily carries a certain political message, as the servitude confirms the legitimacy of the military actions decided by the government.”
The U.S. made a game
In addition to carefully modified, censor-prepared recordings, there are also amateur videos on the server, capturing the deaths of the soldiers: in somewhat hypocritical, "officialized" promotional films at the same time the officers talk about the losses at the most, but the bloody form of the fight does not perform.
After all, there have already been a lot of videos from the battlefields in Iraq and Afghanistan, which were created during sharp deployment, but the British army has only just arrived.
U.S. is recruiting in a slightly different way - using a shooting game called America's Army, which is free to download, and in which the player can, through more or less faithful simulation, get directly into the skin of the soldiers.
