The budget is an essential part of the policy, but it is hidden behind the government's power competition with the opposition.
That's more personalally grateful, and that's why there's a lot more attention involved in the media.
Disgusting politicians is much more interesting to the public than austere numbers.
State budgets in past social-democratic governments have suffered three ailments, which he is not able to solve at the same time:
The first of them is a high share of mandatory, so-called mandatory expenses, which are not only pensions, but also social benefits, which were approved by the silent "coalition" of the ČSSD and KSČM last year before the elections.
The second is the deficit economy as a consequence of the apobility of the first.
The third limit is public finance deficits, i.e. the deficit of budgets of towns and municipalities.
However, the greatest risk is not the deficit of the state budget.
It is public and relatively under control.
However, a time bomb is a deficit of budgets of towns and villages.
Many mayors, in addition to money from taxes, fees and loans from the banks, to obtain additional resources for investments, often recklessly issue bonds that disproportionately indebted their town halls.
In recent years, they have managed to repay these debts through the mass sale of shares of energy and other power grids, which they received from the state in privatization.
They don’t have this source anymore.
If we only stick to official and verifiable facts, we will find that:
Our economy has been undergoing a growth phase for several years, as well as the rest of the world, thereby steadily flaunting the CSSD.
This was a unique opportunity for her governments to put public finances in order.
Instead, the state became more and more indebted.
Other countries, with a few exceptions such as Hungary, Albania, Pakistan or Egypt, managed much more sensuously than our republic.
It's a huge disgrace, which falls mainly on past governments.
We are among the countries that are, on a global scale, the least threatened by poverty.
Therefore, there is no need for massive social programs, which, unfortunately, just in recent years have unnecessarily swelled into gigantic dimensions.
Even if part of the nonsensical populist spending agreed before the elections has been abolished this year, so if there is no shift in the near future the boundary of retirement and pension reform, then in de facto any policy in this country will actually end, and whoever governs it will be a mere incumbent.
With little exaggeration, it can be said that the ruling politicians will become clerks, mechanically distributing most taxed money for pensions and social benefits.
That's why this year's budget can't be revolutionary, but it can only suspend the displacing of normal spending by mandatory spending.
A planned deficit of $70 billion for 2008 would be useless if previous governments did not manage what they could in the last ten years.
If we don’t really want the ruling politicians to become just officials, then we have to be patient and indulge this not-so-strong Coalition government, which realizes that the state can only spend as much as it manages to obtain in taxes and fees, calmness to prepare further reform laws.
Only these will allow a gradual achievement of a balanced state budget.
