The banks want to solve the crisis
The U.S. Federal Reserve, the European Central Bank and the central banks of Britain, Switzerland and Canada have jointly decided to combat the crisis in the financial markets.
More than a hundred and a billion dollars will go to the currency markets through auctions.
In these auctions, the bank is auctioning an interest rate, for which they are willing to borrow money.
The U.S. Fed sends forty-billion-dollar dollars to the auction, then the next twenty-four will be made available to European and Swiss central banks.
It’s after the 11th-September attacks for the first time that national central banks have agree on such a co-ordinated procedure to prevent or reduce the effects of the financial crisis.
"This step is intended to address the greatest difficulties of the economy, i.e. a growing lack of free capital that banks could lend," Bernard Bauman of the consulting company Economic Outlook Group wrote in a report.
"The coordinated action of central banks has lowered the likelihood of a recurrence in the United States from 65 per cent to about 50 per cent," Bau said.
Negotiations have already lost sixty-million-dollar dollars for non-permachiled loans, mainly in the United States.
Analysts are awaiting even more billion-dollar casualties.
"Central banks really have to be afraid of what may still follow when they have opted for this course of action," said ABN Chief Economist Kieran Davies.
"It looks like the situation is really bad," he added.
The central banks are concerned that if banks themselves have difficult access to the loans, they will also lend to their customers.
This would downfuse consumption and send the U.S. economy into a resumption with possible effects on economic growth around the world.
The central banks have thus decided, after failing to reduce the so-called LIBOR graduation, the London inter-bank rate, which is used to lend money in the interbank market.
This meant that the banks were still worried about borrowing each other, even though U.S. central banks, Canada and Britain lowered their interest rates several times during the autumn.
"It's going to have the same effect as a further fall in interest rates," Greg Salvaggio of Tempus Consulting told Reuters.
Some analysts don’t believe that central bank’s streace will contribute to overwhelming the crisis because it doesn’t solve the underlying problem, and that’s the collapse of the U.S. real estate market.
