Allies in Afghanistan Are Passive, U.S. Says
Feather troops and the lack of necessary equipment, according to the United States, are influencing the course of a mission in Afghanistan.
According to Defense Minister Robert Gates, NATO should outline a concrete plan for the next few years.
The mission in Afghanistan does not take place according to its ideas, agreed in Edinburgh, Scotland, by representatives of the countries that have troops in the southern part of the country.
In addition to the growing resistance on the part of the Taliban movement, eight participating states are now discussing further plans and coordinating allies.
While the United States this week, through Defense Secretary Robert Gates, called on NATO troops for greater activity, Great Britain sees a priority in strengthening the authority of the Afghan government and intensiving development aid.
Developments in Afghanistan are palpable, but the military force can only be one part of the solution.
“Another is to enable residents to kick-start the further development of the economy and security themselves,” British Defense Minister Des Browne told the BBC.
Ninety percent
According to U.S. military, the activity of the Afghan insurrection is the highest in the last five years.
The number of violent actions has risen by 27 per cent compared to last year, in the southern province of Helmand even by 60 per cent.
The NATO countries have about 40,000 troops in Afghanistan, about a quarter of those in the southern territory.
According to the U.S., however, the allies reached the very limit in the organization and equipment of the troops.
Specifically, they are asking for additional helicopters, soldiers and experts for the training of the Afghan troops.
"We're going to have to seriously reflect on the concrete targets for the next time and the ways to achieve them," Gates said.
Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop also spoke in a similar sense today.
“I don’t enjoy what we have on the ground and in the air in Afghanistan.
We have about ninety per cent of the troops and equipment we would need, and that's not enough," De Hoop told Reuters at the conclusion of a three-Day talks with the Japanese government in Tokyo.
Who's Passive
In recent months, there has also been controversy among the countries surveyed of whether all allies are doing their utmost in Afghanistan.
Germany, for example, has been criticized for passivity.
"The Germans have a relatively numerous representation in Afghanistan.
However, they are deployed in areas where there is a minimum of violence, and the fighting demands on them are not as high as us in the southern and eastern parts of the country," Gates said.
The British tabloid Sun this week labeled German soldiers as lazy.
"Affghani serpents uncovered: the Germans are playing while our boys are fighting," Sun wrote in the material, complemented by snapshots of German soldiers playing table tennis.
Germany's Bild cross-at-tack and imprinted a picture of a British unit during a rugby match in the southern part of Afghanistan.
The national debt will rise to a trillion-dollar in 2009
For every Czech, the national debt is over 84,000 kroner.
The total debt rose to CZK 84.2.7 billion at the end of September.
The government planned to borrow 159.2 billion crowns this year.
At the beginning of December, however, the ministry reduced the plan to CZK 152.8 billion thanks to better developments in the management of the state budget.
The national debt of the Czech Republic rose to CZK 842.7 billion at the end of September.
In the nine months of this year, it has thus increased by CZK 40.2 billion.
However, it has fallen by 13.4 billion crowns since June.
The Treasury Ministry thus confirmed its data on the state debt at the beginning of October today.
There is a debt of more than 84,000 crowns for every Czech.
For the whole of last year, the state became more than CZK 111 billion.
"For now, it seems that thanks to the lower deficit for the next year, we could surpass the trillion-dollar threshold only in 2009.
Next year the debt should be between 900 billion and a trillion kroner," David Marek, an analyst at Patria Finance, told iHNed.cz.
Vladimír Pikora, an analyst at Next Finance, sees it similarly: "We could get past one-thiron in the course of 2009.
Exactly when it will depend on the next budget we don't already know.
If it turned out that the situation on the political scene would have changed, politicians would be willing to conserve more, it could be postponed to 2010.
But I don't anticipate this change," he said.
Debt grows
The state-majority debt consists of the central government's debts and arises primarily by accumulating state budget deficits.
The sovereign debt is financed by treasury bills, government bonds, direct loans or, for example, loans from the European Investment Bank.
So far this year, the internal indeblation of the state has risen by 38.5 billion to CZK 719.4 billion.
So far this year, the state's foreign debt has risen by 1.7 billion crowns to 123.3 billion crowns.
The so-called tradable sovereign debt reached CZK 803.3 billion at the end of September and has fallen by CZK 13.7 billion since June.
The average repayment of the national debt has risen to 6.5 years.
“Operate debt increased by 13 per cent year-on-year year-to-first to the third quarter, which is really a great deal for how the economy is doing.
Interestingly, the surge is generated mainly for bonds, where there is an increase of 20 per cent.
“It’s bad that the debt grows, but on the other hand, it’s going to show that it’s going to do what it’s going to do when there’s a pension reform,” Pikora said.
Better than expected
It follows from the strategy of financing and managing the state debt for 2007 that the government has planned to borrow CZK 159.2 billion this year.
At the beginning of December, however, the ministry reduced the plan to CZK 152.8 billion thanks to better developments in the management of the state budget.
The amount consists mainly of the deficit of the state budget, which will probably amount to 76 billion crowns this year instead of the expected CZK 91.3 billion.
Furthermore, these are planned maximum resources for the Czech Consolidation Agency 13.3 billion crowns, a ten-billion-dollar reserve for the payment of pensions, the financing of government bonds for 43 billion crowns and repayments of loans from the European Investment Bank of CZK 1.8 billion.
A woman to appear in court in Khartoum today for giving her name to a teddy bear
A British teacher, sentenced in Sudan for an insulating an immemorial, was sentenced to a fifteen-per-driving prison and expiled on Thursday evening as her lawyer said.
The British woman has been accused of having six- to seven-Year-year-old pupils given the name Mohammed, despite the fact that any depiction of the prophet of Islam is forbidden.
She was sentenced by whips.
She was imputed at the Unity High School in Khartoum, Britain's Fifty-two-year-old teacher on Sunday and accused of insulting Islam and rebellion, in accordance with Article 125 of the Sudanese Penal Code, after the parents of several pupils filed a complaint with the Judicial Ministry of Education.
In fact, our story begins as early as the month of September, when the intrusor in question asks their six to seven-year-old pupils to choose a name for a class teddy bear.
"E-eight names will come out of the children's mouth, among them Hasan, Abdullah and Mohammed," the school's director Robert Boulos says.
After voting takes place in the class, 20 out of 23 children will eventually pick Mohammed as the most popular name.
Of course Mohammed is the name of the Muslim prophet, but above all it is a widespread first name in Sudan.
The children then co-resulted a book for their class with a picture of a teddy bear accompanied by the caption: "My name is Mohammed".
The bear himself did not wear a label with this name, but it is difficult to portray a prophet considered an offense of Islam.
Gillian Gibbons, who teaches at the same school in Sudan, has clarified that he knows her well and that she would never have done anything that could be offensive to any religious belief: “I only regret asking the children to vote for a name”, according to him, it is merely an “innocent miscreation”.
Such an opinion is also shared by many female teachers, who describe it as "very attentant to their pupils" and add that "very in the classroom they do not talk about religion".
Recacall that the baptismal name Mohammed is also the second most commonly awarded baptist name in the United Kingdom, in English it is written in fourteen different ways.
This baptismal name often selected by Muslim families is a way to honor a prophet (often awarded to the firstborn).
However, what are the rules regarding the use of this baptized name?
For example, is it possible to give them to a toy?
Of course, it's a matter of interpretation.
In the last round of negotiations this Wednesday in Vienna, they did not reach an agreement on the provincial status.
In the end, failure.
The Serbs and Kosovo Albanians ended on Wednesday morning in Baden, Austria's Baden, the sixth and final round of the talks, without being able to overcome different perspectives on this separatist province.
“It is pitiful that both sides have not been able to come to a status agreement,” European Negotiator Wolfgang Ischinger said during a press conference in Vienna.
His U.S. counterpart, Frank Wisner, stressed that peace in the area is "at stake" and appealed to Belgrade and Pristina in order to take care of their commitments and be ready for dialogue even after the end of the negotiations.
"We do not expect violence in the near future, but we are entering into a difficult period," he added, adding that "it is evident that the situation is tense".
Negotiation ends December 10
Negotiators of Troika, composed of representatives of the European Union, the United States and Russia will last go to Kosovo on Monday.
The future of the province, 90 per cent of Albany-speaking population, could turn out in a brighter light on December 10, when Ischinger, Wisner and Russian negotiator Alexander Bocan-Kharchenko are due to submit their report to the U.N. Security Council.
Conversations in Baden, not far from Vienna, represent the sixth and at the same time the final round of negotiations since the United Nations, in August of this year, inflicted the troika by renegotiation.
U.S. and Europeans are counting that the talks will conclude on December 10.
On the other hand, the Russians, who have previously blocked the granting of the independence of Kosovo in the United Nations Security Council, on the other hand, are insisting that the negotiations continue even after the latest deadline.
"Russia was and remains inclined to a negotiated solution, a compromise solution," Bocan-Charchenko said.
Unilateral promulgation of independence?
After Eight Years of Governance of the International Community, the West sees the independence of Kosovo under the oversight of the EU as the only viable solution.
Pristina declares that in the event that the Security Council does not decide in its favour, it is ready to declare independence unilaterally.
Belgrade maintains widespread autonomy.
"Unfortunately, we have not reached an agreement," the president of the Kosovo self-government Fatmir Sejdiu announced at the Baden hotel, where from Monday these "negotiations last chance took place".
Hashim Thaçi, a former insurgent commander of the Kosovo Albanians and a future premier of the province, expressed remorse.
However, he added that the plan of autonomy introduced by Belgrade "alongs the past, which led to murders and deportations."
"Domino effect"
Serbian President Boris Tadić, on the other hand, believes that a compromise is still under consideration and warned the Albanian majority in Kosovo against the consequences of any unilaterally proclaimed independence of the province.
Bělehrad is said to be particularly concerned about the "domino effect" in an area that is still unstable as a result of wars for independence in the 90's.
"However, this does not mean for Serbia", he said "that the compromise is impossible."
On Tuesday the Serbian delegation insisted that all the decisions belong exclusively to the Security Council, in which its Russian ally possesses veto power.
"Kosov Parliament will have the final say"
"Only a solution arising from the Security Council is acceptable for Serbia," Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Koštunica said.
"These negotiations lasted two years.
It has to come to an end as it has begun: on the floor of the Security Council."
Fatmir Sedjiu told reporters that "the Kosovo parliament will have a last word about the status status, adding that if there was a one-sided declaration of independence by the Albanian-speaking inhabitants, there would have been a "complicity" with the West.
Whale made a deal on Israelis and Palestinians
Speaking at the opening of an international peace conference in Annapolis, Maryland convened at his instigation, U.S. President George W. Bush announced that the Israelis and Palestinians had finally agreed to a joint statement.
Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmud Abbas have agreed to immediately begin negotiations with the incumbent dispute between the two nations.
In the presence of the two men, Bush vowed to do his best to help them conclude a "peace covenant" before the end of 2008.
In the meantime, the Palestinians must destroy "terrorist infrastructures" and Israel has to put an end to colonial activities, Bush said, recalling the obligations for both sides stem from a "travel-to-treat-to-measure" peace they received in 2003.
The head of the White House emphasized that negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian self-government led by Abbas aimed "closing a peace treaty that would solve all undetermined issues undetermined without exception, including central issues."
This statement includes three issues at the heart of a dispute between the Israelis and the Palestinians: the future border of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem, and the fate of Arab refugees expelled in the 1948 conflict from their homes on the territory of today's state of Israel.
Abbas took the U.S. president's address, emphasizing that the follow-up negotiations were "quitotal and thorough" and to touch on all aspects of the solution, saying that he mentioned "Jerusalem, refugees, borders, colonies, water, security, among other things."
Olmert spoke at the grandstand, expressing an indication that subsequent negotiations should be "direct and continuous" and that it is necessary to "grow itself to be concluded in the course of 2008".
Bush determined the end-of-year of 2008 as a date for the realization of his "vision of two states", Palestinian and Israeli, existing side-by-by-should.
Saudi Arabia has revealed that it had made a commitment from Bush in this sense before it decided to participate in the conference.
Saudi Arabia, whose participation in Annapolis is a success for Washington, has initiated a proposal adopted by all the Arab countries, which offers peace to Israel in exchange for the abandonment of all the Arab territories seized during the 1967 war, including East Jerusalem.
Annapolis means "a crucifixion in the history of the Middle East, especially because it fights against the roots of the conflict and against its central aspects," Prince Saoud argues in an interview he gave to an Arabic-speaking diary backed by Saudi capital.
Prosecutors, according to the Attorney, the police are not responsible.
Exactly what happened on Sunday?
As is always the case with such petty news from the Black Chronicle, the official versions do not coincides with what is rumored among people.
What are the circumstances of the accident?
According to the first results of an investigation by the General Inspection of the French State Police (Inspection générale de la police nationale, IGPN), a police car drove through Louise-Michel Street shortly after 17 a.m. in Villiers-le-Bel.
"The vehicle wasn't in an intervention mode, but it was on patrol and was driving at 40 to 50 km/h, it didn't have sidelights turned on," the police source explains.
The green Kawasaki motorcycle "unregistered and therefore without the right to ride on public roads" (reacompany below), controlled by two young men without a helmet, passed "very high speed".
"The motorcycle rammed from the left side into a police car after he didn't give priority from the right," a police officer clarified.
A test of a promile of alcohol in the blood carried out on the octal of the vehicle turned out to be negative.
The condition of the car (thwarted anterior, shredded windshield, broken bumpers) is indicative of the sharpness of the impact.
The Prosecutor of the Republic of Pontoise (République de Pontoise) Marie-Thérèse de Givry recently responded very quickly and announced yesterday morning that she had infuriated IGPN instructed to investigate "actions of inadvertent manslaughter and non-providing assistance to persons at risk", which at the same time takes the direction départmentale de la sûreté publique du Val-d'Oise.
On the occasion of an afternoon press conference, the prosecutor's attorney stated that an IGPN-led investigation ruled, at this stage, the responsibility of police officers for the accident.
According to the clerk, three witnesses of the accident, who were interviewed on Sunday evening, confirmed "a version of the police officers, namely, that the mini-engineer approached them relatively quickly from the left".
A grease, unequitably caused by friction of the popid on the ground, showed yesterday that both vehicles remained wedged until the police car was able to brake.
The vehicles were secured and will go through technical expertise.
An autopsy was carried out on the bodies of both victims yesterday.
What Happened Right Thereafter?
Yesterday in Villiers-le-Bel, rumours quickly spread among people that police officers should quickly leave the crime scene, without providing first aid to two youngsters.
IGPN should also exclude "any serious wrongdoing" at this point.
“It’s a more challenging aspect of an investigation, which requires more investigations, with which it is necessary to be very careful,” the police source said.
"Libération" met with an eyewitness for a few moments following an accident.
One of its windows leads directly where the accident took place.
“Immediately together with my cousin, who is a nurse in the hospital, we ran down to try to resurrect them,” the witness recounts.
We couldn’t do anything.”
He then spotted a "clining, totally terrified police officer.
“Two others tried to resurrect one of the lads, and the fourth spoke on the phone.”
In the face of the rapidly growing tension and the certainty of those present that the situation of the youngsters is hopeless, police officers were taken in the car of a colleague who, in the meantime, arrived at the site.
According to the same Witness, the paramedic arrived "about twenty minutes after the departure of the police officers".
They also performed a heart massage, but in vain.
"Don't let anyone say that the police authorities did not come to help the youngsters.
These youngsters were given first aid.
The firefighters arrived immediately.
It’s all was done for their rescue,” the prosecutor said.
Tensions are rising in Pakistan
The Pakistani police on Monday suppressed the first demonstration since the state of emergency was pronounced by General Musharraf, two days before.
In Lahor, in the east of the country, about 2000 lawyers demonstrating measures taken as part of a state of eremperium, lacrimal grenades were injuvenated, according to Witnesses used by riot police.
The latter responded with stones, injuring several police officers.
At Least 350 Lawyers were subpoiled.
Similar scenarios as in Karachi and Rawalpindi, where officers attacked and injouflaged lawyers.
A few should have been summoned.
In Islama-wasa, Supreme Court lawyers called for a general strike and announced demonstrations, as a result of a wave of about five-square-up arrest of opponents, human rights activists and lawyers over the weekend.
Accessory streets to the Supreme Court were damined by roadblocks on Monday morning, in order to prevent the assembly.
Further demonstrations were supposed to take place in Multan (centre) and Peshawar (Northwest).
OnMondday, the movement of lawyers gained the support of religious parties grouped in Muttahid Majlis-e-Amal, whose president "explained the population to send flowers to the judges who opposed the state of emergency and the garbage to those sworn against Musharraf."
"Musharraf's visor doesn't apply"
The Pakistani Supreme Court is the direct target of an emergency measure promulgated Saturday evening.
In the coming days, he was set to comment on the re-election of President Musharraf in an indirect presidential election that took place on October 6.
Judiciary president Iftikhar Mohammad Chaudhry has been eliminated and rescued.
“Everything we are witnessing is unlawful, unconstitutional and against the Supreme Court’s judgments,” An official told The News.
Parvez Musharaf’s promise to relinquish the leadership of the army and become a civilian president, he does not pay for the state of emergency,” Deputy Information Minister Tariq Azeem said on Monday.
It was expected to relinquish the military uniform until November 15, when his first presidential term ended.
The deputy minister of information also confirmed that the January elections could be postponed.
The Pakistani government also refuted ongoing speculation in the country, according to which the deputy head of the army should have ordered President Musharraf's house arrest.
