There will be a marathon at the end of the year on the stock exchange? Experts were divided by 'subprime' crisis
Fewer than five weeks are left until the end of an administrative year, and investors have their hands with me in anticipation of a traditional marathon at the end of the year.
However, this year the experts aren't so sure it's going to happen.
Banif believes that "from a statistical point of view, the month of December has had a positive course on the stock market".
However, it is not appropriate to create predictions solely on the basis of statistics, especially because, in the current context, markets offer, at least in the short term, certain doubts.
These experts don’t expect the stock market to move forward in the last few weeks of the year, stating that “the stock market is sustained by an existence of liquidity, which is ready to invest due to the lack of safeguard alternatives with guarantees, especially a solid income affected by the effect of turnover to high-profile securities, both in long-stay and short sections”.
Therefore, they believe that the stock exchange will have a rather calm course, not without volatility, "particularly because it is not known the impact of a likely slump in Fed (Federal Reserviary System), due to the high levels of oil prices and the dangers of stimulus to inflationary pressures".
Javier Barrio, head of BPI's sale in Spain, agrees that it's not the right time for bold statements that a marathon will take place.
“There is not much cause for joy,” this expert assures.
IBex lost nearly 4 percent of the maximum values recorded on November 8, when the index closed at 15 945.7 points.
However, all experts don't have such a Negative Opinion.
Juan Solana, an Intermoney analyst, believes that Ibex 35 could end a year closer to 16,000 points thanks to reciprocating portfolios and traditional supplementary pension contributions, which occur every year at this time.
However, this expert suggests that everything will be a little different this year, as he believes the marathon will take place sooner, in the second week of December after a long weekend on the occasion of the Constitution Day.
The above-mentioned analyst does not believe that the volatility would affect the direction of the market.
Federico Olucha, an Inversis Banco analyst, estimates that we are approaching the technical levels and "that's why we could observe a reciprocative reflection of an index triggered by U.S. news about the consequences of the crisis in the credit market."
Analyst believes that these consequences will be positive.
Analysts coincide in the selection of titles, which will take care to bring Ibex to the new maximum dimensions.
Intermoney's solona confirms that "there's a great turn this year on strong titles."
This expert would include both large banks and some construction companies in the portfolio, "because when this period comes, the energy industry will be very expensive".
Federico Olucha of Inversis Banco, on the other hand, reassures that the surgeries will go hand in hand with banking and the industrial sector.
The construction companies on this marathon will also be able to be enriched as long as it happens.
“The ACS has been regaining positions for a few months and we hope that other construction companies, such as Ferrovial and FCC, will go the same way,” the expert explains.
Cyclical consumption in the last part of the year will be at the forefront thanks to an increase in sales during Christmas sales.
On the other hand, this expert believes that the technologies will also do very well, even if they are not very much represented in this field on the Spanish market.
Fif times in seven years
In the last seven years, December has only been a month of slumps in just two occasions.
The actual marathon took place at the end of the year in 2003, in an administrative year when the stock markets ended the crisis triggered by the bursting of a technology bubble.
Earlier this year, when Ibex 35 regained 28.17%, December recorded an 6.68% increase.
We’ll see if 2007 will be the fifth profit year (so far Ibex has risen 8.36% since January) and whether it manages to have its marathon at the end of the year.
Two Hundred Branches of the Popular Bank will be open for 12 hours from Friday to Saturday
The Popular bank has decided to modernize most of its branches.
A total of two Hundred, located in the most trading parts of Spain.
These branches operated under the Popular Max brand are a big change for the bank.
Likewise for other Spanish entities, because it is the first group that has decided to make such a jump and bet on the continuous opening hours of its branches until 20.30 a.m. and to open on Saturday from 10 a.m. to 20.30 p.m.
BBVA, another group, which has also assured several times that it wants to extend the opening hours of its branches, has not yet decided on this step.
In the meantime, it intends to conclude an agreement with trade unions to abolish the opening hours of 75% of its branches on Saturday in exchange for an extension of opening hours by a one-in-a-half, albeit not for the public.
The results of this Explanatory experience will be critical of other entities that support the opening hours of transactions should be added or not.
Exactly this is the aim of a project set out by the Mutt Bank.
Obtain individual clients who go to these places for their purchases, smaller and medium-sized companies, sole traders and shops from selected streets, where branches will be opened.
The bank wants to open 200 of these branches throughout Spain within about 18 months, because it is not the opening of new branches, but only an adjustment already existing.
For this reason, the bank will modernize the branches that it already has in the business zones by adapting them to the profile of clients in individual streets.
Thus, the elongation of the opening hours is complemented by a different concept of branch space and a new, more up-to-date design in brighter colors.
Ángel Rivera's business director reassures that this restructuring won't mean a big investment, but more hiring, as it transitions from six employees to a £8- to-nine.
Branches will even be accommodated premises for meetings of small and medium-sized companies and will also sell non-financial products.
Positive experience for the group.
Bank Popular has already tested this initiative in three Madrid branches.
And it was a positive experience, as Ángel Rivera explains.
Branches on the streets of Madrid Bravo Murillo, O'Donnell and Gran Vía have been selected, and according to River, it appears to have been a happy choice.
The Popular bank currently has 2,500 branches.
And an additional 60 specialized immigrants, which are run under the Mundocredit brand also have a very long opening time and open even on Sundays and holidays.
PopularMax branches won't follow the BBVA model, offering a wide range of non-finance products for sale.
“We don’t want to compete with our clients,” Rivera said.
