Their names are on the list from which Swiss director Marcel Schüpbach "Karlin list" (2006, in Spain unreleased), which at the rate of a detective thriller follows from Belgrade to New York the Swiss plaintiff while persecuting fledgling criminals and pushing for relevant states to retract the network.
What kind of insuity is suitable for me?
Insurrection of a car that protects the driver in the event of an accident constitutes a substantial expenditure, which depends on various factors, such as age, status, gender, length of possession of a driving licence, the type of vehicle to be insured or used, etc.
Having a car is just the beginning.
Choosing suitable car insurance is even more difficult than choosing a car model.
First of all, we need to figure out what we are talking about.
Car insurance is an agreement between an insurer and an insured person.
This liability takes the form of an insurance contract, which stipulates that the insurance company receives an agreed insurance amount in a certain amount for taking care of the payment of damages in the event of an insured event.
Insured is the price of insurance, which is determined taking into account the various factors, such as age, status, sex, length of possession of a driving licence, the type of vehicle that is insured or its use, etc.
Insurance companies determine their prices on the basis of stats, so the more likely that there is an accident, the higher the insurance rate.
For this reason, both persons over the age of 25 and women have to pay lower premiums, as statistics show that they have a lower risk index due to their caution.
In the same way on the basis of inspections, insurance companies concluded that those living in a marriage have fewer accidents than single drivers.
The manner in which the vehicle is used will have an impact on the frequency of journeys and thus the likelihood that it will have an accident.
The increase in the frequency of journeys increases the probability.
There are many variables that insurance companies are considering when they set the price of insurance premiums.
That's why the driver must well study the situation in order to choose an insurance that best corresponds to the profile of an insured person.
Here we will show you the most important variables for the choice of this or another type of insurance, which, in addition to satisfying the type of insured, will also be at a sensible price.
People under the age of twenty-five encounter different disadvantages such as little driving experience and short possession of a driver's license when they want to arrange premiums at an affordable price.
Despite the ideal for good coverage is insurance against all the risks, there are also cheaper options.
The precariest drivers can choose insurance against all the risks with a discount of insurance premiums in order to obtain insurance coverage of all risks, but at a more affordable price.
This kind of the contract would assume the collaboration of the client in payment of an insured event.
"Part of the reimbursement of an indemnity provided by the client will be predetermined when determining the price of the premium, which will be all the higher than that which would be the lower participation," explains Luis Noguera, the head of First Europe in Spain, an insurrection intermediary over the Internet.
Another option is the insurance extended to other participants of the operation in such a way that the client is insured against the damage caused to other persons, but not against the damage to his own car.
In addition, it would be insured against the theft, fire and the loss of the vehicle.
Recall recommendations.
If you're under the age of twenty-five, try to be attentive and avoid accidents as much as possible, because it will affect your bonus and make it possible to reduce premiums in the future.
The bonus is to reduce the basic rate that the insurance company has.
On the other hand, malus is an increase in this base rate.
"Bonus and malus will depend on the insurer and its profit.
If the insurer has had a good year, it will be able to afford to increase the bonus,” Noguera explains.
After crossing the twenty-five-year limit, drivers have it easier when they go to take out an insurrection, because the client’s risk index decreases, thanks to the experience gained.
For this reason, it is most recommended to take out insurance against all risks at a discount.
"Young people are usually unable to take advantage of pre-acquired experience at the moment when there is an accident, even while driving," assures the director of a European company in Spain, adding that most of the accidents take place in the second year of management, when the "fear of a newcomer" has already been overcome.
However, if the client is married but does not yet have children, it is advisable to take out insurance against all the risks, as long as his economic situation allows.
Families with children are regarded by insurers as the clientele with the lowest risk index, and therefore their surcharges will be lower.
In this case, the best insurance would be insurance against all risks with a discount of insurance premiums (which could amount to 300 euros) or at a high bonus, insurance engrossed to other business participants, starting from the third year after the purchase of the vehicle.
"Their management is quieter and their purchasing capacity is higher, so they can afford the luxury of getting the highest resumption," notes Luis Noguera.
Retirees are advised to insuge against all risks with a discount of insurance premiums (which amounts to €150) or insurance enriched to other business participants if a high bonus has been achieved.
It has to be said that car insurance is something important not only because it protects the driver in the event of an accident, but also because it constitutes a considerable expense, which is a compelling reason to properly study the possibilities of insurance, which would suit us best in relation to our personal situation.
Celtic tries to score a point at AC Milan, which means eight-finals
AC Milan and Celtic will measure their strength this Tuesday at the San Siro stadium in an attempt to get a point missing to achieve their respective goals at the end of "Champions", as it would have meant being the first for the Italians, and it would have decided the place-position for the Scots.
The Italians have ten points and they've already scored a draw in Portugal's fifth game, while Celtic has nine points, but he needs one more to make sure Shakht'or doesn't get in front of him with six points, who's going to play with Benfica, who's having four.
These two Group-D fights will be played a week before the sixth round, as AC Milan have to play in the Intercontinental Cup in Japan for the first time in the semi-finals, in an attempt to advance to the 16-year-on-on-year final.
So this group will be closed earlier than the others and the indecisive result at Giuseppe Meazza Stadium seems to be the most likely, even though both teams will try to compete for the victory because the Italians will not want to tactiste on home soil and Celtic knows that after the win he would be the first and would secure a better place in the eight-finals.
Gordon Strachan’s team could even make a loss if Shacht'or doesn’t win but can’t reliest on that, because at the same number of nine points the Ukrainians, who lost in Glasgow (2:1) last week but won in the first direct match (2:0).
And so the AC Milan players will try to spoil the evening to the Scots, who are confident that for the second time they will go to the semi-finals in the reorganised Champions League just as they defeated them in the same match in the previous competition with the Kaka goal in overtime (1:0) after a state of 0:0 in the basic playing time.
To hope that they will win the "rossoners" will be Šacht'or, for whom he is worth a victory only if Celtic loses, both for advancing to the eight-finals, as well as for remaining at UEFA.
Benfica, although last and does not have the chance to continue in the top European competition, could achieve third place with one victory, so it would play at UEFA.
The first Spice Girls concert was attended by 20 000 people in Vancouver
The Spice Girls has started their first tour in Vancouver (Canada) in front of 20 000 spectators since it broke up in 2000.
"Posh Spice" (Victoria Beckham), "Scary Spice" (Melanie Brown), "Baby Spice" (Emma Bunton), "Sporty Spice" (Melanie Chisholm) and "Ginger Spice" (Geri Halliwell) shortly before they started their concert at the GM Place Stadium in Vancouver declared that the reunion of their group was "great".
Approximately 20 000 people have purchased tickets so that they could be present at a concert, which also included a video rescribing the band's history, which sparked a sensation in the late 1990s.
The return and world tour take place at the same time, when a new album is being released by EMI in Spain, which brings a selection of their main successful songs, as well as two new songs, "Headlines" and "Voodoo".
Twenty Spice Girls appeared adorned with bracelets on stage, which were donated by the husband "Posh Spice", British footballer David Beckham.
This athlete was in an audience that flooded GM Place, even though a nervous Victoria forbade him a few days ago.
Prior to the start, Victoria Beckham stated about the British star that "he didn't see our costumes at all, so I'm nervous about what he thinks of the show".
"Sporty Spice", whose departure from the group in 1998 "because of contradictions between us" caused the dissolation of a five-member group two years later, she also expressed her nervousness at a meeting with reporters two hours before the performance began.
“I was afraid.
I've been nervous, but I've seen that world public opinion had had changed, my opinion on the Spice Girls has changed.
“And I was thinking why not, so I’m glad I made my decision,” Chisholm said of reuniting a group she had dissolved nine years ago.
The singer also remarked that over the time "things have straightened up and we all love each other very much".
However, a lot of things have evolved since the Spice Girls last performed together in 1998.
So at the outset, according to CTV television, the group flew to Vancouver with a Virgin Airlines special.
An army was awaiting nanny on arrival at the airport.
The five have a total of 7 children.
After the concert in Vancouver, the band will perform in California (U.S.), San José (December 4) and in Los Angeles (December 5 and July).
He will perform in Madrid on December 23.
Concerns about a new quarrence over gas between Russia and Ukraine
Controversy over natural gas prices is currently taking place between Russia-Ukraine, thus increasing the risk that Russian fuel supplies to Western Europe can be affected.
Most Russian fuel exported to the European Union (EU) is conducted through a pipeline through Ukraine, and any dispute between these two countries is sharply pursued.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow raises gas prices for Ukraine, Russia will charge higher transit fees.
The previous controversy between these two last year reduced supplies to EU states.
The latest controversy between Kyiv-Moscow comes at a time when the Ukrainian government and the Russian state-owned gas monopoly, Gazprom, are trying to agree on how much Ukraine will have to pay for Russian gas in 2008.
Gazprom says it will have to charge Ukraine more because it has agreed to pay up to 50 per cent more gas it receives from neighboring Turkmenistan.
Analytists say it could mean that Ukraine will have to pay 40 per cent more for Gazprom supplies.
"It is important for us to use our arguments as one of the most important gas transit states in the course of the negotiations," Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinach told Reuters.
"Changes in gas prices should undoubtedly be associated with our arguments regarding the amount of transit fees (to Western Europe) through Ukraine."
The last major controversy between Moscow and Kyiv caused a reduction in supplies from Gazprom to Ukraine in January 2006.
This caused a crushing fall in exports to Western Europe, which sharply increased prices.
Russian gas accounts for one-square-to-quarter supply to the European Union.
Russia has the world's largest gas reserves, but a large part of it remains underused.
Responsive, Gazprom replenishes its own gas supplies from Turkmenistan and other former Soviet Central Asian republics.
NHS "must control foreign employees"
An inspector said the NHS's Scottish Chamber must improve the checks of criminal records among non-European employees.
Recommendations from the president of the Supreme Audit Office, Robert Black, came after a foreign doctor operating in Scotland was hooked up for June's attacks on Glasgow Airport.
Mr. Black also fears that the Scottish NHS doesn't have an exact idea of a number of foreign personnel it employs.
But his report emphasized that the other key inspections went well on a sample of five health authorities.
Immediately after the attacks at Glasgow Airport in June, the president of the Supreme Audit Office was interested in personnel obtained from outside the European Economic Area.
Twenty-eight-year-old Bilal Abdullah, an Iraqi clerk at Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was detained at the airport after someone drove Jeep into the door of the main terminal.
He was one of the three doctors who appeared in the Central Criminal Court using a video bridge charged with a connection to attempted bomb attacks in the United Kingdom.
NHS chambers are expected to inspect foreign personnel in terms of qualifications, experience and authorisation to work in the United Kingdom prior to his employment.
Although the Scottish health commissions use Disclosure Scotland for criminal charges, the agency has no power to obtain such information outside the UK.
The report of the President of the Supreme Audit Office says, among other recommendations that commissions should be guided by Criminal Records Bureau directives, which assists employers with inspections in several countries.
"Although we have found that part of the personnel's records contained evidence of such checks, they are not carried out on a regular basis," the report said.
Mr. Black has said that the Scottish NHS is in many ways coinciding with its own pre-employment review policy, but also added: "Our report draws attention to the areas in which the system could be improved in order to ensure the availability of accurate information on a number of foreign personnel."
The chairman of the BMA of Scotland Dr. Peter Terry said it was "very unlikely" that pre-employment controls would identify potential NHS employees who could commit an act of terrorism in the future.
"The report indicates that current pre-employment checks at the Scottish NHS work well and it is uncleatable what further value those comments will bring," he said.
